410→                           Erdős proved that $1/2$ is possible and Krückeberg <a href="#cite-Kr61-329" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('Kr61','329'); return false;">[Kr61]</a> proved $1/\sqrt{2}$ is possible. Erdős and Turán <a href="#cite-ErTu41-329" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('ErTu41','329'); return false;">[ErTu41]</a> have proved this $\limsup$ is always $\leq 1$. <br><br>The fact that $1$ is possible would follow if any finite Sidon set is a subset of a perfect difference set (see <a href="/44" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer ugc" target="_blank">[44]</a> and <a href="/707" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer ugc" target="_blank">[707]</a>).<br><br>This question can also be asked for $B_2[g]$ sequences (i.e. where the number of solutions to $n=a_1+a_2$ with $a_1\leq a_2$ is at most $g$ for all $n$, so that a $B_2[1]$ set is a Sidon set). Kolountzakis <a href="#cite-Ko96-329" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('Ko96','329'); return false;">[Ko96]</a> constructed a $B_2[2]$ sequence where the $\limsup$ is $1$, and for larger $g$ constructions were provided by Cilleruelo and Trujillo <a href="#cite-CiTr01-329" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('CiTr01','329'); return false;">[CiTr01]</a>.
